course: dictionary
lesson: 9339
title: A word for breathing holes
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Insects have no lungs, so how do they breathe? The text says air enters «through little holes along the sides called spiracles». The word called tells you these holes have a special name, and the sentence tells you what they are for.
explain: Read on for more clues: the air «travels down branching tubes to every part». A spiracle is the doorway where air gets in, and the tubes carry it inside the body. Try the swap: "air enters through little holes along the sides called breathing holes" still makes sense, so breathing hole is a good meaning.
explain: Fish use this word too. In sharks and rays a spiracle is an opening on the head, and a whale's blowhole is sometimes called a spiracle. The job is the same, breathing, but the place on the body is different.
fact: A spiracle is a tiny breathing hole.
fact: Insects and spiders breathe through spiracles along the sides of their bodies.
fact: «Insects have no lungs.» Air goes in through spiracles and down tiny tubes that carry oxygen to the cells.
fact: Caterpillars have a row of spiracles that you can see on their sides.
fact: Rays use spiracles behind their eyes to take in water while they lie on the sea floor.
exercise: «Insects have no lungs: air enters through little holes along the sides called spiracles.» What is a spiracle? | A hard, hollow shell | *A tiny breathing hole | A kind of insect food | A sort of wing
exercise: Where can you see the spiracles on a caterpillar? | On its head only | Inside its mouth | *Along its sides | Under its feet
exercise: Which animal uses a spiracle behind its eyes to take in water? | A bird | A cat | *A ray | A butterfly
test: Why can an insect breathe without lungs? | *Air enters through spiracles and travels down tubes | It has large fins to swim | It holds its breath all day | It breathes through its mouth only
test: In a whale, what is sometimes called a spiracle? | Its tail | *Its blowhole | Its fin | Its eye
exam: Which clue in «air enters through little holes along the sides called spiracles» helps you work out what a spiracle is? | The word along | The word called only | *The words little holes that air enters through | The word insects
